      title: "Похвала скучной климатической политике",
      text: "Climate politics has a weakness for the spectacular. Summits end with pledges to reach net zero by a date comfortably beyond the tenure of anyone signing them. Billionaires announce schemes to pump aerosols into the stratosphere. Activists warn, not without reason, that we are approaching a tipping point after which the thawing of the permafrost will release vast quantities of methane and make further warming self-sustaining. Against this backdrop, it can seem almost frivolous to argue that what the world needs most is not drama but tedium. Yet that is precisely my argument.\n\nConsider the less glamorous instruments of decarbonisation. Building regulations that require landlords to retrofit insulation and install heat pumps. A modest levy on fuel, rising predictably every year. Planning rules that allow a wind farm to be approved in months rather than a decade. Upgrades to the electricity grid so that power generated in windy coastal regions can actually reach the cities that need it. None of these will ever dominate a news cycle. Collectively, however, they account for a far larger share of emissions cuts than any headline-grabbing initiative.\n\nAdmittedly, boring policy has its own problems. Predictable carbon pricing, whether through a straightforward tax or a cap-and-trade scheme, raises costs for households, and governments that introduce it often face a fierce backlash. France learned this painfully when a planned rise in fuel tax triggered months of protests. Granted, too, that incremental measures may simply be too slow given the scale of the crisis. Were we starting in 1990, gradualism might have sufficed; in the 2020s, some argue, only radical change will do.\n\nBe that as it may, radical change that cannot survive the next election is no change at all. The strongest argument for unglamorous policy is durability. Measures embedded in law, administered by competent agencies and designed to incentivise private investment create expectations on which businesses can plan. An energy company deciding whether to build a gas plant that will operate for forty years needs to know whether that plant will become a stranded asset. Binding targets and stable rules answer that question; grand but vague pledges do not.\n\nThis is also why greenwashing flourishes in the absence of dull, enforceable standards. When the rules are fuzzy, companies are free to make impressive claims about carbon offsetting that bear little relation to their actual emissions. Clear reporting requirements, by contrast, leave little room for creative accounting.\n\nNone of this means abandoning ambition. Carbon capture, rewilding and even, in time, carefully regulated forms of geoengineering may all play a role. But they should complement the slow, unspectacular work of mitigation, not distract from it. History suggests that societies are transformed less often by grand gestures than by thousands of sensible decisions that nobody bothered to report. The climate transition is unlikely to be the exception.",
