    text: "For most of human history, the shape of a society's population resembled a pyramid: many children at the base, progressively fewer people at each older age, and a narrow peak of the very old. Within a single lifetime, that pyramid has begun to turn into something closer to a column and, in some countries, an inverted pyramid. This transformation, driven by falling birth rates and rising life expectancy, is arguably the most predictable of all global challenges. It is also one of the least discussed.\n\nThe figures are striking. In 1950, there were roughly twelve people of working age for every person over sixty-five worldwide. Today that ratio stands at around seven to one, and by 2050 it is projected to fall to around four. In Japan, Italy and South Korea, the change is even more dramatic: within two decades, there will be fewer than two working-age adults for every pensioner. South Korea's fertility rate, at well under one child per woman, is among the lowest ever recorded.\n\nThe economic consequences follow almost inevitably. A shrinking workforce means slower growth, unless productivity rises to compensate. Fewer taxpayers must fund the pensions and healthcare of a growing number of retirees, whose needs, in turn, become more expensive as chronic conditions accumulate in later life. Governments have responded by raising the retirement age, a measure that is fiscally sensible but politically explosive, as repeated protests in France have shown.\n\nYet it would be a mistake to treat ageing purely as a burden. Older people contribute enormously to society, not least through unpaid work: caring for grandchildren, volunteering and supporting other family members. Many would happily remain in paid employment for longer if work were organised more flexibly, and employers facing labour shortages have strong incentives to make this possible. Moreover, an ageing population is, among other things, evidence of success: of better medicine, safer workplaces and improved living standards.\n\nMigration offers a partial solution. Younger workers from countries with growing populations can fill gaps in care homes, hospitals and construction sites. However, migration on the necessary scale is politically contentious in many receiving countries, and it raises uncomfortable questions about the departure of skilled workers from the poorer countries that trained them. It is also, in the long term, only a temporary fix: birth rates are now falling almost everywhere, including in much of Africa and South Asia.\n\nUltimately, adapting to ageing will require changes on many fronts at once. Health systems will need to shift their emphasis from hospitals towards prevention and care at home. Cities will need housing and transport designed for people in their eighties and nineties. And attitudes to work and retirement will have to become more flexible, so that the decades added to our lives are years of contribution and fulfilment rather than mere dependency.\n\nThe pyramid is not coming back. The question is whether we design societies that fit the shape that is replacing it.",
